<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pentateuch, Five Books of Moses, Written Torah, Chumash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Torah is the compilation of the first five books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy, also known as the Pentateuch or the Five Books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Rabbinic tradition holds that its teachings were given by God through the prophet Moses, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> and at the Tabernacle; in rabbinic literature the word also covers the Oral Torah, now embodied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> and Midrash. Biblical scholarship generally sees its final form as largely complete by the Persian period and based on earlier sources. Written on a scroll by a scribe, it is read publicly in synagogues, and it is also sacred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torah.jpg" alt="Torah" width="330" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Тора._Нацыянальная_бібліятэка_Беларусі.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They believe in angels, in the scriptures revealed before the Quran, among them the <b>Torah</b> and the Gospel, in God’s decree and in the Day of Judgement, when the dead will be raised and judged and enter paradise or hell.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…made a covenant with Abraham, promising him descendants and a land; his grandson Jacob, renamed Israel, was the father of twelve tribes; the Israelites, enslaved in Egypt, were freed under the leadership of Moses, received the <b>Torah</b> from God at Mount Sinai and entered the land of Canaan.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible, the Tanakh, is the foundational work of Hebrew literature: the <b>Torah</b>, the historical and prophetic books, the Psalms, the wisdom of Proverbs and Job, and the poetry of the Song of Songs, composed and edited over many centuries and reaching its final form in the…</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…king Cyrus the Great allowed the exiles to return in 538 BCE, the Temple in Jerusalem was rebuilt and dedicated around 515 BCE, and the community of the Persian province of Yehud took shape around it, governed by the <b>Torah</b>, the law of Moses, which became its constitution.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…six psalms with a blessing before and after, is recited on the first days of Passover, on Shavuot, on all seven days of Sukkot, on Shemini Atzeret and Simchat <b>Torah</b>, and on the eight days of Hanukkah; the sages gave a short word, &quot;BeBeTaCh&quot;, for remembering the days.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li><li><blockquote>Some religious Jews criticised it for naming neither God nor the <b>Torah</b>; Rabbi Abraham Isaac Kook wrote an alternative, &quot;HaEmunah&quot; (&quot;The Faith&quot;), while still endorsing the original.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li></ul></section>
